Zopfli spends time deciding how many bits to use for each symbol: common symbols get shorter codes, rare symbols get longer ones. This is called Huffman coding.

The old code always used a complicated algorithm that enforces a maximum code length. Profiling showed it consumed about one-third of the compression time.

The optimization works like this:

1. Build the optimal Huffman codes using a simpler, faster algorithm.
2. Check whether every code fits within the required length limit.
3. If it does, use those codes. Otherwise, run the original algorithm.

About 88% of nontrivial cases passed that check, avoiding the expensive algorithm.

This preserves compression quality: the simpler algorithm already finds optimal codes, and the original algorithm handles cases needing the limit. Matching the original tie-breaking rules also kept our tested output exactly identical.

The general principle is: measure the expensive work, then find a cheaper way to produce the same result for common cases. Here, that reduced total runtime from 22.08 to 16.96 seconds.

